<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Great Xi</h1><p class="st-meta">1644 CE – 1647 CE · capital Chengdu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The short reign of the rebel Zhang Xianzhong in Sichuan, notorious in Chinese history for mass killings during the collapse of the Ming.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-xianzhong">Zhang Xianzhong</a>, a former soldier from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, led one of the great peasant armies that roamed northern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from the 1630s. In 1644, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, Zhang invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> and proclaimed himself king of Great Xi.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Zhang set up ministries, examinations and coinage, but his regime is remembered above all for terror against officials, scholars, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> and ordinary people suspected of disloyalty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan's</a> population collapsed during these years through massacre, war, famine and plague; how much Zhang himself was responsible is still debated, as later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing</a> accounts exaggerated.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In late 1646 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hooge">Hooge</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-xianzhong">Zhang Xianzhong</a> was killed in battle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xichong-county">Xichong</a> in early 1647. His adopted sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-kewang">Sun Kewang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-dingguo">Li Dingguo</a> led his army south and later served the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/great-xi.jpg" alt="Great Xi" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southern_Ming.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1644 CE – 1647 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chengdu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zhang Xianzhong</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Mandarin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Xi (Zhang Xianzhong)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1646&amp;focus=great-xi" aria-label="Great Xi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7376 1059 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Great Xi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7696 1315L7567 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7567 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7696 1315h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7696 1315h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chengdu. <a href="/atlas?year=1646&amp;focus=great-xi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

